ciIdeal -- the ideal of a list of conditional independence statements
*********************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        ciIdeal (R, Stmts)
        ciIdeal (R, G)
        ciIdeal (R, Stmts, PlayerNames)
        ciIdeal (R, G, PlayerNames)
  * Inputs:
      * R, a *note ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,, must be created using
        probabilityRing
      * Stmts, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, the list of conditional
        independence statements
      * G, a *note graph: (Graphs)Graph,, the graph modelling the conditional
        dependencies between players
      * PlayerNames, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, the ordered list of
        players - the names of the random variables in the conditional
        independence statements or vertices of the graph. If PlayerNames is
        omitted, the players (or the vertices of G) are assumed to be labelled
        1..n.
  * Outputs:
      * an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, the ideal in R of conditional
        independence relations

Description
===========

ciIdeal computes the ideal of a list of conditional independence statements.
The input can be the list of conditional independence statements itself, or a
graph modelling the conditional dependencies between players. This method is
the same as *note conditionalIndependenceIdeal:
(GraphicalModels)conditionalIndependenceIdeal, from the *note GraphicalModels:
(GraphicalModels)Top, package, included here for convenience and compatibility
with this package.

A single conditional independence statement is a list consisting of three
disjoint lists of indices for random variables, e.g. $\{ \{1,2\},\{4\}, \{3\}
\}$ which represents the conditional independence statement "$(X_1, X_2)$ is
conditionally independent of $X_4$ given $X_3$". Given an undirected graph $G$,
the conditional independence statements are produced via the globalMarkov
function from the GraphicalModels package. A global Markov statement for $G$ is
a list $\{A, B, C\}$ of three disjoint lists of vertices of $G$, where the
subset $C$ separates the subset $A$ from the subset $B$ in the graph $G$.

The output is an ideal of the given ring PR, which must be created using the
probabilityRing function. This function computes the ideal using the *note
conditionalIndependenceIdeal: (GraphicalModels)conditionalIndependenceIdeal,
function from the GraphicalModels package, then maps it to an ideal of PR via
the mapToProbabilityRing function.



