        format that are the payoff tensors of a random game

Description
===========

The list $Di$ represents the format of the game. This example creates a random
game of format $2 \times 2$.



+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : X = randomGame({2,2});                             |
+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : peek X#0                                           |
|                                                        |
|o2 = Tensor{coefficients => QQ                         }|
|            format => {2, 2}                            |
|            indexes => {{0, 0}, {0, 1}, {1, 0}, {1, 1}} |
|                      3                                 |
|            {1, 0} => -                                 |
|                      4                                 |
|                      7                                 |
|            {1, 1} => -                                 |
|                      4                                 |
|                      9                                 |
|            {0, 0} => -                                 |
|                      2                                 |
|                      9                                 |
|            {0, 1} => -                                 |
|                      4                                 |
+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : peek X#1                                           |
|                                                        |
|o3 = Tensor{coefficients => QQ                         }|
|            format => {2, 2}                            |
|            indexes => {{0, 0}, {0, 1}, {1, 0}, {1, 1}} |
|                       7                                |
|            {0, 1} => --                                |
|                      10                                |
|                       7                                |
|            {1, 0} => --                                |
|                      10                                |
|                      7                                 |
|            {0, 0} => -                                 |
|                      9                                 |
|                      7                                 |
|            {1, 1} => -                                 |
|                      3                                 |
+--------------------------------------------------------+

The optional argument CoefficientRing allows to change the ring of payoffs. If
no coefficient choice is specified, the payoffs will be rational numbers. This
example creates a random game of format $2 \times 2$ with integer coefficients.

+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : X = randomGame({2,2}, CoefficientRing => ZZ);      |
+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : peek X#0                                           |
|                                                        |
|o5 = Tensor{{0, 0} => 6                                }|
|            {0, 1} => 0                                 |
|            {1, 0} => 2                                 |
|            {1, 1} => 6                                 |
|            coefficients => ZZ                          |
|            format => {2, 2}                            |
|            indexes => {{0, 0}, {0, 1}, {1, 0}, {1, 1}} |
+--------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : peek X#1                                           |
|                                                        |
|o6 = Tensor{{0, 0} => 9                                }|
|            {0, 1} => 3                                 |
|            {1, 0} => 5                                 |
|            {1, 1} => 6                                 |
|            coefficients => ZZ                          |
|            format => {2, 2}                            |
|            indexes => {{0, 0}, {0, 1}, {1, 0}, {1, 1}} |
+--------------------------------------------------------+

Outputs of this function can be used as input for the functions *note
nashEquilibriumIdeal: nashEquilibriumIdeal_lpRing_cmList_rp,, *note
spohnMatrices: spohnMatrices,, *note spohnIdeal: spohnIdeal, and *note
konstanzMatrix: konstanzMatrix,.

